course: dictionary
lesson: 3656
title: Firstly, secondly, finally
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Firstly» is a word that shows order. When a writer gives reasons or steps, it tells the reader which one comes first. «Firstly, children need time to eat.» The reader knows this is the first reason, and more will follow.
explain: Writers often use «firstly» with «secondly», «thirdly» and «finally» or «lastly». These words are like counting on your fingers, so the reader can follow the argument or the instructions from start to finish.
explain: In information texts, headings such as «Habitat», «Diet» and «Life cycle» work like signposts. Words like «Firstly», «Secondly» and «Finally» work the same way. They tell you where you are, like signs in a big shop that show you which aisle to walk down.
fact: «Firstly» is an adverb. It means first, or in the first place.
fact: «Firstly» introduces the first point in a list of reasons or steps.
fact: The words that usually come next are «secondly», «thirdly», and «finally» or «lastly».
fact: Many writers just use «first», which means the same thing and is shorter.
fact: Signposts such as «Firstly» help a reader follow an argument or instructions.
exercise: In «Firstly, children need time to eat.», which word shows this is the first point? | time | children | *Firstly | need
exercise: After «firstly», which word would a writer use for the second point? | *Secondly | Yesterday | Quietly | Under
exercise: What does «firstly» mean? | Used to ask a question | Used to describe a colour | Used to end a story with a happy ending | *Used to introduce the first point in a list
test: A writer has given two reasons and wants to end the list. Which word fits best? | Yesterday | Under | *Finally | Firstly
test: In an information text, what do headings and signposts like «Firstly» do? | They tell the reader the author's name | They make the text longer and harder | They are pictures on the cover of the book | *They help the reader find their way through the text
exam: Which word starts a list of reasons, so the reader knows the first point is coming? | Lastly | *Firstly | Thirdly | Finally
